1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,230
Section 1

2
00:00:03,230 --> 00:00:04,322
Amber:

3
00:00:04,322 --> 00:00:06,910
Hello William, this is Amber.

4
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:11,691
You said to phone if I wanted to get more information about the job

5
00:00:11,691 --> 00:00:13,660
agency you mentioned.

6
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,680
Is now a good time?

7
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:16,367
William:

8
00:00:16,367 --> 00:00:18,490
Hi Amber, yes, fine.

9
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:23,570
So, the agency I was talking about is called Bankside, they're based in Docklands.

10
00:00:23,570 --> 00:00:27,560
I can tell you the address now, 497 Eastside.

11
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:28,304
Amber:

12
00:00:28,304 --> 00:00:28,907
Ok, thanks.

13
00:00:28,907 --> 00:00:33,530
So, is there anyone in particular I should speak to there?

14
00:00:33,530 --> 00:00:33,580
William:

15
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:37,460
The agent I always deal with is called Becky Jamieson.

16
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:37,510
Amber:

17
00:00:37,510 --> 00:00:40,271
Let me write that down.

18
00:00:40,271 --> 00:00:40,740
Becky...

19
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:43,367
William:

20
00:00:43,367 --> 00:00:48,620
Jamieson, J-A-M-I-E-S-O-N.

21
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:49,020
Amber:

22
00:00:49,020 --> 00:00:51,420
Do you have her direct line?

23
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:51,850
William:

24
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:54,430
Yes, it's in my contact somewhere.

25
00:00:54,430 --> 00:01:01,720
Right, here we are, 07866 510333.

26
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:04,800
I wouldn't call her until the afternoon if I were you.

27
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:09,210
She's always really busy in the morning trying to fill last minute vacancies.

28
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:10,586
She's really helpful and friendly,

29
00:01:10,586 --> 00:01:14,440
so I'm sure it would be worth getting in touch with her for an

30
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:14,990
informal chat.

31
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:15,391
Amber:

32
00:01:15,391 --> 00:01:19,800
It's mainly clerical and admin jobs they do, where isn't it?

33
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:20,200
William:

34
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:21,000
That's right.

35
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,690
I know you're hoping to find a full-time job in the media eventually.

36
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:28,317
But Becky mostly recruits temporary staff for the finance sector,

37
00:01:28,317 --> 00:01:32,670
which will look good on your CV and generally pays better too.

38
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:33,074
Amber:

39
00:01:33,074 --> 00:01:38,330
Yeah, I'm just a bit worried because I don't have much office experience.

40
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:38,595
William:

41
00:01:38,595 --> 00:01:39,390
I wouldn't worry.

42
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:43,110
They'll probably start you with a receptionist or something like that.

43
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:48,017
So, what's important for that kind of job isn't so much having business skills

44
00:01:48,017 --> 00:01:50,470
or knowing lots of different computer systems.

45
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:52,790
It's communication that really matters.

46
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:56,684
So, you'd be fine there and you'll pick up office skills really quickly on

47
00:01:56,684 --> 00:01:57,240
the job.

48
00:01:57,240 --> 00:01:58,980
It's not that complicated.

49
00:01:58,980 --> 00:01:59,507
Amber:

50
00:01:59,507 --> 00:02:00,560
Okay good.

51
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:05,240
So how long do people generally need temporary staff for?

52
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:09,370
It would be great if I could get something lasting at least a month.

53
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:09,656
William:

54
00:02:09,656 --> 00:02:11,084
That shouldn't be too difficult,

55
00:02:11,084 --> 00:02:14,797
but you're more likely to be offered something for a week at first,

56
00:02:14,797 --> 00:02:15,940
which might get extended.

57
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:18,930
It's unusual to be sent somewhere for just a day or two.

58
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:19,304
Amber:

59
00:02:19,304 --> 00:02:24,538
Right, I've heard the pay isn't too bad, better than working in a shop

60
00:02:24,538 --> 00:02:25,660
or a restaurant.

61
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:26,025
William:

62
00:02:26,025 --> 00:02:27,120
Oh yes, definitely.

63
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,780
The hourly rate is about 10 pounds, 11 if you're lucky.

64
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:31,242
Amber:

65
00:02:31,242 --> 00:02:32,630
That's pretty good.

66
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:36,900
I was only expecting to get 8 or 9 pounds an hour.

67
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:37,160
William:

68
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:40,280
Do you want me to tell you anything about the registration process?

69
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:40,685
Amber:

70
00:02:40,685 --> 00:02:41,495
Yes, please.

71
00:02:41,495 --> 00:02:44,330
I know you have had an interview.

72
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:44,598
William:

73
00:02:44,598 --> 00:02:46,472
The interview usually takes about an hour,

74
00:02:46,472 --> 00:02:49,150
and you should arrange that about a week in advance.

75
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:49,496
Amber:

76
00:02:49,496 --> 00:02:53,300
I suppose I should dress smartly if it's for office work.

77
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:56,460
I can probably borrow a suit from mum.

78
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:56,816
William:

79
00:02:56,816 --> 00:03:00,730
Good idea, it's better to look too smart than too casual.

80
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:01,077
Amber:

81
00:03:01,077 --> 00:03:05,940
Will I need to bring copies of my exam certificate or anything like that.

82
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:06,166
William:

83
00:03:06,166 --> 00:03:08,430
No, they don't need to see those, I don't think.

84
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:08,874
Amber:

85
00:03:08,874 --> 00:03:10,650
What about my passport?

86
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:10,896
William:

87
00:03:10,896 --> 00:03:12,860
Oh yes, they will ask to see that.

88
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:13,425
Amber:

89
00:03:13,425 --> 00:03:13,990
Ok.

90
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:14,267
William:

91
00:03:14,267 --> 00:03:16,480
I wouldn't get stressed about the interview there.

92
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:19,420
It's just a chance for them to build relationship with you.

93
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:22,280
So, they can try and match you to a job which you like.

94
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:28,480
So, there are questions about personality that they always ask candidates, fairly basic ones.

95
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:32,934
And they probably won't ask anything too difficult like what your plans are for

96
00:03:32,934 --> 00:03:33,570
the future.

97
00:03:33,570 --> 00:03:34,140
Amber:

98
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:35,850
Oh, hope not.

99
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:36,197
William:

100
00:03:36,197 --> 00:03:39,670
Anyway, there are lots of benefits to using an agency.

101
00:03:39,670 --> 00:03:44,250
For example, the interview will be useful because they'll give you feedback on your

102
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:44,577
performance,

103
00:03:44,577 --> 00:03:46,540
so you can improve next time.

104
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:46,934
Amber:

105
00:03:46,934 --> 00:03:50,480
And they'll have access to jobs which aren't advertised.

106
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:51,034
William:

107
00:03:51,034 --> 00:03:54,360
Exactly, most temporary jobs aren't advertised.

108
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:54,721
Amber:

109
00:03:54,721 --> 00:03:59,780
And I expect finding a temporary job this way takes a lot less time.

110
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:03,730
It's much easier than bringing up individual company.

111
00:04:03,730 --> 00:04:04,185
William:

112
00:04:04,185 --> 00:04:05,094
Yes, indeed.

113
00:04:05,094 --> 00:04:07,821
Well, I think I've covered everything...

114
00:04:07,821 --> 00:04:08,730
(fade out)
